// In-depth battles, batch a: Megido, Kadesh, Muye, Maratona, Termopilas, Salamina.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "carro de guerra": "A light two-wheeled cart pulled by two horses, with a driver and a warrior (sometimes two). It was the elite weapon of the Bronze Age: fast, expensive and terrifying.",
  "faraó": "The king of ancient Egypt. He was seen as a god on earth and led the army in person.",
  "anais": "A record of events, year by year. Thutmose III's were carved on the walls of the temple at Karnak.",
  "cerco": "Surrounding a fortified city and cutting off its food and water until it surrenders, instead of attacking it head on.",
  "divisão": "A large part of an army that marches and fights under its own commander. The Egyptian army at Kadesh had four, each named after a god.",
  "mandato do céu": "A Chinese idea that Heaven gives the right to rule to a just king and takes it away from a cruel one. It served to explain (and justify) the change of dynasties.",
  "vassalo": "A people or lord who obeys a stronger king, pays tribute and sends soldiers when called, but keeps its own government.",
  "hoplita": "A heavy infantry soldier of the Greek cities: a big round shield (the hoplon), spear, helmet and bronze breastplate. He fought shoulder to shoulder with his neighbors.",
  "estratego": "An Athenian general. In 490 BC, Athens elected ten a year, one per tribe, and they took turns in command.",
  "polemarco": "An Athenian magistrate who, in 490 BC, still held authority over the army and the place of honor on the right wing.",
  "trirreme": "A Greek warship with three rows of rowers on each side, about 170 in all. Light and fast, its main weapon was the ram.",
  "esporão": "A bronze point fixed to the bow of a warship at the waterline. It was used to pierce the hull of the enemy ship.",
  "retaguarda": "The back of an army. Being attacked from behind is almost always the end of a formation."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  megido: {
    war: "Thutmose III's first campaign in Canaan (c. 1457 BC)",
    hook: "The oldest battle told in detail, and the shortcut a pharaoh chose against all his generals.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Around 1457 BC, Egypt was living at the start of the New Kingdom, its period of greatest power. To the north, in Canaan and Syria, there were dozens of city-kingdoms that paid tribute to the [[pharaoh|faraó]]. Farther away lay the kingdom of Mitanni, Egypt's rival, which encouraged those cities to rebel.",
        "When Queen Hatshepsut died, the young Thutmose III began to rule alone. Almost at the same time, the king of Kadesh gathered a coalition of Canaanite and Syrian cities and occupied Megiddo, a fortress that controlled the main road between Egypt and Mesopotamia.",
        { h: "The road to Megiddo" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Year 22 of the reign", "Thutmose sets out with the army from the Egyptian border at Tjaru."],
          ["About 10 days later", "He reaches Gaza and continues north."],
          ["Year 23", "At Yehem, he holds a war council and chooses the Aruna road."],
          ["The day after the crossing", "Battle before Megiddo. Then, seven months of siege."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Thutmose III" },
        "He had spent about twenty years in the shadow of Hatshepsut, his aunt and stepmother. This was his first great campaign as sole king, and he wanted a quick and complete victory. Over his reign he would make at least 16 campaigns and take Egypt as far as the Euphrates. Many historians call him the greatest general of ancient Egypt.",
        { h: "The king of Kadesh" },
        "The Egyptian text does not give his name, only his title. He was the leader of the revolt, backed by Mitanni, and commanded a coalition of local kings, among them the king of Megiddo. His strategy was to defend the exits of the most likely roads, near the city.",
        { h: "Tjaneni, the scribe" },
        "Almost everything we know comes from him. Tjaneni was the military scribe who accompanied the army and kept a diary. Some twenty years later, a summary of that diary was carved into Thutmose's [[annals|anais]] on the walls of the temple of Amun at Karnak."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to estimates drawn from the Egyptian account, Thutmose had between 10,000 and 20,000 men, and the coalition between 10,000 and 15,000. Many modern historians think both sides had similar numbers, around 10,000, with about a thousand [[chariots|carro de guerra]] each.",
        { note: "The numbers do not appear directly in the annals. They are modern reconstructions, and they vary widely." },
        { h: "The three roads" },
        "To reach Megiddo one had to cross the Carmel ridge. There were three roads: one in the north, leaving near Zefti and Yokneam; one in the south, through Taanach; and one in the middle, through the Aruna pass, today's Wadi Ara. The middle one was the shortest, but so narrow that, according to the annals, the horses had to pass one behind the other, and the soldiers too.",
        "The generals warned: if the enemy waited at the exit of the pass, the vanguard would be slaughtered while the rear was still far behind. Thutmose listened and decided the opposite. He himself would go first, by the Aruna road, and whoever wished could take the others.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "The king of Kadesh was waiting for the enemy on the wide roads, and the pass was left unguarded. The Egyptian army came out the other side without a fight. Thutmose waited until the last troops had passed and camped on the plain, by the Qina brook, in front of the city.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "The next morning, Thutmose drew up the army in three wings, in a concave line that threatened both enemy [[flanks|flanco]]. According to the annals, the southern wing stood on a hill south of the Qina brook (another translation of the sentence says Taanach); the northern one, northwest of Megiddo. The king himself rode in the center, in a chariot of electrum (a natural alloy of gold and silver).",
        "The Egyptian attack broke the Canaanite line quickly. According to the annals, the enemies abandoned their horses and chariots of gold and silver and ran for the city. The gates were already shut, and those inside hauled the fugitives up the walls, lifting them by their clothes.",
        { h: "The mistake" },
        "Here the Egyptian text itself admits a stumble: instead of pursuing the enemy and entering the city, the soldiers stopped to plunder the enemy camp. The annals say that, if not for this, Megiddo would have fallen that day. Without the quick victory, a [[siege|cerco]] began: the Egyptians surrounded the city with a ditch and a wooden palisade."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Megiddo surrendered after about seven months. The annals list the plunder: 340 prisoners, 2,041 mares, 924 chariots, 200 suits of armor, 502 bows, 1,929 head of cattle and 22,500 sheep. The king of Kadesh managed to escape, and Thutmose would have to return to Syria many times. The defeated Canaanite princes swore loyalty to the pharaoh.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It is the first battle in history told with reliable detail: roads, war council, positions and plunder. It also shows an idea that generals would repeat for millennia: the hardest road can be the safest, precisely because nobody expects it. In 1918, the British general Allenby defeated the Ottomans in the same region and received the title Viscount Allenby of Megiddo.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: the Egyptian account gives year 23 of Thutmose's reign. In modern years, many settle on 1457 BC, but other chronologies propose 1482 or 1479 BC." },
        { note: "Armageddon: the name comes from the Hebrew Har Megiddo, 'mount of Megiddo'. The Book of Revelation chose the place for the final battle, perhaps because so many fights had already happened there." },
        { note: "The plunder lists in the annals (prisoners, mares, chariots, cattle) are numbers from the Egyptian scribe himself, and English Wikipedia marks the list as doubtful. Treat them as an order of magnitude." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  kadesh: {
    war: "Wars between Egypt and the Hittites over Syria (13th century BC)",
    hook: "A perfect ambush, a pharaoh surrounded in his own camp, and both sides swearing they had won.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 13th century BC, two superpowers contested Syria: Egypt, to the south, and the Hittite Empire, with its capital at Hattusa, in the center of today's Turkey. Between them lay rich cities that changed sides with the wind. Kadesh, on the Orontes river, was the most important: whoever held it controlled the road to the north.",
        "Ramesses II came to the throne young and wanted to recover the lands Egypt had lost. In year 4 of his reign, he brought the kingdom of Amurru, on the coast, over to the Egyptian side. The Hittite king Muwatalli II answered by assembling an enormous army with allies from all over his empire.",
        { timeline: [
          ["Year 4 of Ramesses", "Campaign on the coast; Amurru switches from the Hittites to Egypt."],
          ["Year 5, spring", "Ramesses sets out with four divisions toward Kadesh."],
          ["Year 5, day 9 of the 3rd month of Shemu", "Battle of Kadesh (probably May 1274 BC)."],
          ["c. 1258 BC", "Egypt and the Hittites sign a peace treaty."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Ramesses II" },
        "He was about 25 years old. He would go on to reign 66 years, have dozens of children and fill Egypt with giant temples, such as Abu Simbel. At Kadesh, he made a serious mistake out of haste and then showed personal courage to save the situation. He himself made sure all of Egypt heard only the second part.",
        { h: "Muwatalli II" },
        "The Hittite king, older and experienced. He set a textbook trap: he hid the army behind the city and sent false spies. But, at the decisive moment, he kept most of his infantry out of the fight, and nobody knows for sure why."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Egyptian army had four [[divisions|divisão]], named after gods: Amun, Ra, Ptah and Seth, perhaps 5,000 men each, plus a force called the Ne'arin, who came by another road along the coast, and Sherden mercenaries. Muwatalli had, according to estimates, about 2,500 [[chariots|carro de guerra]] and between 15,000 and 40,000 foot soldiers. The Hittite chariots carried three men; the Egyptian ones, two, and were lighter.",
        { note: "The numbers come almost entirely from Ramesses' inscriptions, and he had an interest in making the enemy look gigantic. Modern scholars use them with caution." },
        { h: "The trap" },
        "Near Kadesh, the Egyptians captured two Shasu nomads, who said the Hittite king was far away, in Aleppo, afraid of the pharaoh. Ramesses believed them and ran ahead with the division of Amun, camping northwest of the city. The other divisions followed behind, spread out over kilometers.",
        { formation: true },
        "Already in camp, the Egyptians caught two Hittite scouts and beat them until they told the truth: the entire Hittite army was there, hidden behind 'old Kadesh', on the other side of the river. Ramesses sent messengers to hurry up the divisions at the rear. It was too late.",
        { h: "The blow" },
        "The Hittite chariots crossed the Orontes south of the city and fell from the side on the division of Ra, which was marching in column, expecting no attack. The division fell apart. The fugitives ran toward the camp of Amun, with the Hittite chariots on their heels, and panic spread. The Hittites broke through the camp's palisade.",
        { h: "Ramesses surrounded" },
        "Here comes the most famous part of the Egyptian account. Ramesses, with his personal guard, mounted his chariot and attacked several times. The official poem says he fought alone and that the god Amun gave him strength; that is propaganda, but it is likely that the king really did fight in person. What saved him was something else: many Hittite chariots stopped to plunder the camp, too rich to ignore.",
        { h: "The turning point" },
        "At that moment the Ne'arin force arrived, coming from the coast, and caught the scattered Hittites. Muwatalli sent about a thousand more chariots of [[reserve|reserva]] across the river. The Egyptians, now reinforced, made six charges and pushed the Hittite chariots against the Orontes. Many drowned trying to cross. The division of Ptah arrived at dusk; that of Seth, only after the battle.",
        { note: "The Egyptian reliefs show the prince of Aleppo being pulled from the river upside down, to vomit the water. It is one of the liveliest scenes in Egyptian art." },
        "Muwatalli, on the other side of the river, never sent in his infantry. The next day, according to some reconstructions, there was a second, inconclusive fight. Ramesses did not have the strength to besiege Kadesh and went back to Egypt."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "On the battlefield, a draw: Ramesses escaped destruction and inflicted heavy losses. In strategy, the Hittites won: Kadesh remained theirs, Amurru returned to their side, and they advanced as far as the region of Damascus. The Hittite documents speak of a victory of their own.",
        "Ramesses had his version carved in at least five temples: Abydos, Luxor, Karnak, Abu Simbel and the Ramesseum, in a poem and in a 'bulletin'. That is why Kadesh is one of the best-documented battles of Antiquity, and also one of the first great propaganda campaigns.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "About 15 years later, Ramesses and the new Hittite king, Hattusili III, signed a treaty of peace, mutual aid and the return of fugitives. It is the oldest peace treaty whose text we know. A clay-tablet version is in the Istanbul museum, and a replica stands at the UN headquarters in New York. Ramesses later married a Hittite princess.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Probably the largest chariot battle in history, with perhaps 5,000 to 6,000 chariots in total." },
        { note: "Why did Muwatalli not use his infantry? Perhaps because it could not cross the river in time, perhaps because it was meant to defend the city. The sources do not say." },
        { note: "Who the Ne'arin were is debated: elite Egyptian troops or Canaanite mercenaries. The word seems to come from a Semitic term for 'young warriors'." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  muye: {
    war: "Conquest of the Shang dynasty by the Zhou (c. 1046 BC)",
    hook: "The day Heaven, according to the winners, changed owners.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "For more than five centuries, the Shang dynasty ruled the Yellow River valley. It was the Shang who left the oldest Chinese writing we know, carved on bones used for divination, and spectacular ritual bronzes. To the west, in the Wei river valley, lay Zhou, a [[vassal|vassalo]] people that kept growing stronger.",
        "According to tradition, the last Shang king, Di Xin (also called King Zhou of Shang, a name unrelated to the Zhou people), was cruel and lived in orgies, surrounded by his concubine Daji. King Wen of Zhou gathered allies, and his son, King Wu, decided to attack.",
        { timeline: [
          ["Before the war", "King Wen of Zhou expands his network of allies."],
          ["c. 1048 BC", "According to tradition, Wu gathers allies at the Mengjin ford on the Yellow River, but turns back: the moment had supposedly not yet come."],
          ["c. 1046 BC", "Wu returns, crosses the river and marches to Muye, near the Shang capital."],
          ["Day of the battle", "The Shang are defeated; the capital, Yin, is taken."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "King Wu of Zhou" },
        "The son of King Wen, he inherited his father's project. Tradition portrays him as patient: he waited for the right sign before attacking. He died a few years after the victory, and his brother, the Duke of Zhou, governed as regent.",
        { h: "Di Xin, the last Shang king" },
        "Almost everything we know about him was written by his enemies or by those who inherited their version. He was described as strong and intelligent, but a tyrant. Part of the Shang army was far away, fighting the Yi peoples in the east, when the Zhou attacked.",
        { h: "Jiang Ziya" },
        "Adviser and general to King Wu, also called Taigong. A poem in the Shijing, the Book of Odes, compares him to an eagle at the king's side. He became a figure of legend and novels in China."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The traditional texts give the Zhou about 45,000 foot soldiers and 300 [[chariots|carro de guerra]], plus about 3,700 chariots from allies, from peoples such as the Yong, Shu, Qiang, Lu and Pu. To the Shang they give about 170,000 men; one text even says 700,000. These numbers are from the chronicles, written centuries later, and almost certainly exaggerated.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        "Muye was a plain south of the Shang capital, in what is now northern Henan province. Before the fighting, King Wu gave a speech to the soldiers, preserved in the Book of Documents as the 'Oath at Mu', in which he accused Di Xin of listening to his wife, abandoning the sacrifices to the ancestors and mistreating the people.",
        "Di Xin, with the main army far away, is said to have hastily armed slaves and prisoners. According to the Zhou sources, many of them did not want to fight: they fled or changed sides. The Zhou chariots attacked, and the troops loyal to the Shang king were crushed.",
        "Not all sources tell of an easy victory. The 'Wucheng' chapter of the Book of Documents says that enough blood flowed to float the pestles (some translations say logs). The philosopher Mencius, centuries later, refused to believe this: for him, a just king would not meet resistance that justified such slaughter. The Yi Zhou Shu, on the other hand, describes a brutal conquest, with prisoners and the massacre of civilians on entering Yin.",
        { h: "The end of the king" },
        "According to tradition, Di Xin fled to his tower, the Deer Terrace, put on his jewels and killed himself in the fire. Another text, the Yi Zhou Shu, says King Wu killed him with an axe. Daji was also killed, and the heads of both were displayed on a spear."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The capital Yin fell, and the Zhou dynasty began, lasting about 800 years, the longest in Chinese history. The Zhou let a son of Di Xin govern part of the Shang lands, to look after the ancestors, but he later rebelled and was defeated.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "To justify overthrowing a king, the Zhou created a powerful idea: the [[mandate of heaven|mandato do céu]]. Heaven gives power to whoever governs well and takes it from whoever governs badly. Every later Chinese dynasty, until 1912, used this idea, and so did every victorious rebel.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date is much debated: scholars have proposed at least 44 different years, between 1130 and 1018 BC. The official Chinese chronology project adopted 1046 BC, supported by astronomical and radiocarbon calculations, but the choice is criticized." },
        { note: "The bronze Li gui vessel was made by a Zhou official, a week after the conquest (on the xinwei day, eight days after the jiazi day). The inscription says King Wu attacked the Shang on the morning of a jiazi day, in the Chinese calendar, and adds a phrase with the character sui. Some read it as Jupiter well placed in the sky; others, as a ritual. Scholars have not reached agreement. It is one of the few records from the period." },
        { note: "The image of Di Xin as a monster is the winners' version. Later scholars, such as Wang Chong, already questioned the accusations, and in the Analects (19.20) Confucius' disciple Zigong says that the wickedness of Zhou (Di Xin) was not as great as people told." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  maratona: {
    war: "First Persian invasion of Greece (492–490 BC)",
    hook: "Ten thousand citizens ran against the greatest empire in the world and got home in time to save the city.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Around 500 BC, the [[Achaemenid|aquemênida]] Persian Empire, under King Darius I, stretched from Egypt to India. The Greek cities of Ionia, on the coast of today's Turkey, lived under its rule. In 499 BC, they revolted, and Athens and Eretria sent ships to help. The rebels burned Sardis, one of the Persian capitals.",
        "The revolt was crushed in 494 BC. According to Herodotus, Darius asked a servant to repeat to him three times a day, at dinner: 'Master, remember the Athenians.' It was time for revenge.",
        { timeline: [
          ["499–494 BC", "Ionian Revolt, with the support of Athens and Eretria."],
          ["492 BC", "First Persian expedition; part of the fleet is wrecked near Mount Athos."],
          ["490 BC", "New expedition, across the Aegean Sea. Naxos is taken; Eretria falls through treachery, according to Herodotus on the seventh day of the siege."],
          ["September 490 BC", "The Persians land in the bay of Marathon, about 40 km from Athens."]
        ]},
        "On board was Hippias, the former tyrant of Athens, expelled 20 years earlier. It was he who suggested Marathon: a plain good for cavalry and close to the city. The Persians counted on his supporters inside Athens."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Miltiades" },
        "An Athenian aristocrat who had governed the Chersonese, in today's Turkey, as a vassal of Darius himself. He knew the Persians from the inside. He was one of the ten [[strategoi|estratego]] and the one who insisted most on fighting.",
        { h: "Callimachus" },
        "The [[polemarch|polemarco]] of Athens. The ten strategoi were divided, five for fighting and five against. According to Herodotus, Miltiades persuaded Callimachus to cast the tie-breaking vote. Callimachus commanded the right wing and died in the battle.",
        { h: "Datis and Artaphernes" },
        "The Persian commanders. Datis was a Median noble and an experienced admiral; Artaphernes, a nephew of King Darius. They did not need to win at Marathon: it was enough to hold the Athenians there while Hippias' supporters opened the city."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Athenians had about 9,000 to 10,000 [[hoplites|hoplita]], plus about 1,000 from Plataea, the only city that came to help. Athens asked Sparta for help, sending the runner Pheidippides (or Philippides), who, according to Herodotus, reached Sparta the day after leaving. The distance is calculated at more than 200 km (books give from 200 to 240 km). The Spartans replied that, because of a religious rule, they could only march out at the full moon.",
        "The Persians would have had some 20,000 to 30,000 fighters, according to modern estimates (many cite 25,000 infantry and 1,000 cavalry), including archers, light infantry and cavalry. Later ancient sources speak of 200,000 to 600,000, a number with no basis. The fleet is said to have had 600 triremes, according to Herodotus.",
        { h: "The terrain and the wait" },
        "The plain of Marathon lies between mountains and the sea, with a marsh at each end. The Greeks camped in a sanctuary of Heracles, up high, blocking both roads to Athens. For five days, nobody attacked: the Greeks did not want to come down to the plain and face the cavalry; the Persians did not want to go up.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "The standoff ended when the Greeks attacked. An old explanation, from a Byzantine dictionary, says that 'the cavalry was away': the Persians had supposedly begun to embark their horses to attack Athens by sea, and the Greeks took advantage. There is no certainty.",
        "Miltiades knew that, if the Greek line were shorter than the Persian one, it would be surrounded. So he stretched the line to the same width, leaving the center with few ranks and reinforcing the two ends. The Athenians stood by their ten tribes; Callimachus on the right, the Plataeans on the left.",
        "Herodotus says the distance between the armies was eight stadia, close to 1.5 km, and that the Greeks covered it running. Many modern historians doubt a run of 1.5 km in heavy armor and suppose that only the last meters, under the arrows, were at a quickened pace. It is a hypothesis, not something from the sources.",
        "In the center, the Persians and the Sacae, their best troops, break the thin line and push the Greeks inland. On the ends, the reinforced hoplites win and the Persian wings flee. Instead of pursuing, the two Greek wings turn inward and attack the Persian center from the sides.",
        "Surrounded, the Persian center falls apart and runs for the ships. Many die in the marsh. On the beach, the Greeks try to hold the boats by the stern and capture seven. There died Cynegeirus, brother of the poet Aeschylus, his hand cut off as he grabbed a ship.",
        { h: "The run back" },
        "The Persian fleet rounded Cape Sounion to attack an unprotected Athens. According to Herodotus, someone on land made a signal with a shield flashing in the sun, and to this day it is debated who. The Athenians marched back the same day and arrived before the ships. Seeing the army already on the beach at Phaleron, Datis gave up and returned to Asia."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to Herodotus, 6,400 Persians and 192 Athenians died. The Athenian number is usually considered reliable: the dead were buried in a collective tomb, the Soros, which is still at Marathon, and Pausanias says the names were carved on stelae. Modern estimates for the Persians are lower, in the range of 4,000 to 5,000. The Plataeans and the slaves who fought are said to have been buried separately, according to Pausanias. The Spartans arrived the next day, saw the dead and went away.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first time Greek hoplites defeated the Persians in open field, and proof that they could do it. The generation of Marathon became a model in Athens: Aeschylus, on his own epitaph, preferred to remember that he fought at Marathon rather than speak of his tragedies. Ten years later, the Persians would return much larger, at Thermopylae and Salamis.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The marathon: the story of the messenger who ran from Marathon to Athens, shouted 'We have won!' and died appears only centuries later, in Plutarch and Lucian. Herodotus tells of another run, Pheidippides' to Sparta. The Olympic event was born in 1896, inspired by the legend." },
        { note: "The date: the Spartan full-moon rule allows the battle to be calculated in August or September 490 BC. August Böckh's calculation (1855) gives 12 September, the most repeated, but it is a reconstruction; Portuguese Wikipedia gives 10 September, and some accept 12 August if the Spartan calendar was a month ahead." },
        { note: "The Persian numbers and the role of the cavalry remain debated. Herodotus does not say where the cavalry was during the battle." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  termopilas: {
    war: "Second Persian invasion of Greece (480–479 BC)",
    hook: "Three days, a goat path and the most famous defeat in history.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Darius died without avenging Marathon. His son, Xerxes I, spent years preparing the rematch: he had a canal dug at Mount Athos, so the fleet would not be wrecked as in 492 BC, and bridges of boats built over the Hellespont, the strait between Asia and Europe.",
        "Many Greek cities surrendered. About 30, led by Sparta and Athens, formed an alliance. The plan was to stop the Persians by land at Thermopylae and by sea at Cape Artemisium, at the same time, because the Persian army depended on the fleet for food.",
        { timeline: [
          ["Spring 480 BC", "Xerxes' army crosses the Hellespont."],
          ["Summer 480 BC", "The Greeks occupy Thermopylae and Artemisium."],
          ["End of summer", "Three days of fighting at Thermopylae; naval fighting at Artemisium at the same time. Proposed dates range from July to September."],
          ["Weeks later", "Athens is occupied and burned."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Leonidas I of Sparta" },
        "One of the two kings of Sparta, already about 60 years old, by estimate. He took only 300 Spartans, all with living sons, so that no family would die out. Sparta was celebrating the religious festival of the Carneia and the Olympic Games were under way, and the bulk of the army was supposed to come later. According to Herodotus, an oracle had said that Sparta would lose its city or a king.",
        { h: "Xerxes I" },
        "King of Persia since 486 BC. He commanded the largest army yet assembled. He waited four days, thinking the Greeks would flee on seeing its size. They did not flee.",
        { h: "Ephialtes" },
        "A Greek from the region, from Trachis. In exchange for a reward, he showed the Persians the mountain trail. His name became the word for 'nightmare' in modern Greek."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Herodotus totals 5,200 hoplites: 3,100 from the Peloponnese (300 Spartans, 500 from Tegea, 500 from Mantinea, 120 from Orchomenus, 1,000 from other Arcadian cities, 400 from Corinth, 200 from Phlius and 80 from Mycenae), plus 700 from Thespiae, 400 from Thebes and 1,000 Phocians. The Opuntian Locrians came with all the men they had, without a number. Diodorus speaks of about 7,400, and modern estimates are around 7,000, counting the light troops.",
        "Herodotus says Xerxes had about 2.6 million fighters, an impossible number. Modern historians propose from 120,000 to 300,000, and some far fewer, from 40,000 to 65,000.",
        { note: "Herodotus' Persian numbers are, without doubt, exaggerated. The logistics of the time could not support even a fraction of that." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Thermopylae means 'hot gates', because of the sulfurous springs. At the time, the sea came much closer to the mountain, and the pass was only a few meters wide in some places. At the middle point, the Phocians had built an old wall, which the Greeks repaired. Today the coast has advanced several kilometers because of silting.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "Day 1" },
        "Xerxes first sent in the Medes and Cissians, then the [[Immortals|imortais]]. The hoplites took turns in front of the wall, one city at a time. In the narrow pass, the big shields and the longer spears were worth more than numbers. The Spartans pretended to flee, the Persians ran after them in disorder, and the Spartans turned and killed them. Xerxes, says Herodotus, leapt up three times from his throne, anxious for his army.",
        { h: "Day 2" },
        "The same again. No attack got through. Xerxes did not know what to do, until Ephialtes appeared with the information about the Anopaea trail, over the mountain.",
        { h: "The night and day 3" },
        "At night, the Immortals, commanded by Hydarnes, climbed the trail. In the morning, they found the 1,000 Phocians who guarded the path. The Phocians withdrew to a hill thinking they were the target, and the Persians simply kept going.",
        "Runners warned Leonidas. He sent most of the allies away and stayed with the 300 Spartans, about 700 Thespians, who refused to leave, and 400 Thebans, who Herodotus says stayed by force, as hostages. The helots, Spartan serfs, probably stayed too.",
        "On the last day, the Greeks left the wall and fought in the widest part, to kill as many as possible. Leonidas died, and there was a fierce fight over his body. When the Immortals appeared in the [[rear|retaguarda]], the survivors gathered on a small hill, the Kolonos. There they died under the arrows. The Thebans surrendered."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Persian victory. Herodotus speaks of up to 20,000 Persian dead and 4,000 Greeks killed; historians consider both numbers too high. The Greek fleet left Artemisium, and Xerxes occupied Boeotia and Attica. Athens was burned.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Militarily, it was a defeat. But it won time, gave the Greeks morale and showed that the Persians could be held. Salamis came weeks later. For the Greeks, and for the West after them, Thermopylae became the symbol of fighting to the end for a cause.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The famous sayings, such as 'Come and take them' (about the weapons) and 'We will fight in the shade' (about the arrows that would block out the sun), come from Plutarch (Herodotus attributes the 'we will fight in the shade' one to the Spartan Dienekes, not to Leonidas). They may be true or Spartan legends." },
        { note: "The epitaph of Simonides, carved at the site, said in short: 'Stranger, go tell the Spartans that here we lie, obeying their laws.'" },
        { note: "The 300 are only part of the story: the Thespians died in the same proportion and are almost forgotten. On the hill of Kolonos, the archaeologist Spyridon Marinatos found Persian bronze arrowheads in 1939, which match the account of the last stand." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  salamina: {
    war: "Second Persian invasion of Greece (480–479 BC)",
    hook: "Athens was already in flames when its fleet saved Greece in a tight strait.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Thermopylae, the road to Athens was open. The Athenians abandoned the city: the women, children and old people were taken to the island of Salamis, to Aegina and to Troezen. The Persians occupied the Acropolis and burned it.",
        "Three years earlier, Themistocles had persuaded Athens to use the silver of a new mine, at Laurium, to build a fleet of [[triremes|trirreme]]. An oracle at Delphi had said that a 'wooden wall' would save Athens. Themistocles explained: the wall was the ships.",
        { timeline: [
          ["483–482 BC", "Athens uses the silver of Laurium to build about 200 triremes."],
          ["Summer 480 BC", "Thermopylae and Artemisium."],
          ["September 480 BC", "Athens is occupied; the Greek fleet gathers at Salamis."],
          ["September 480 BC", "Battle of Salamis."],
          ["479 BC", "Greek victories at Plataea and Mycale."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Themistocles" },
        "An Athenian politician of modest origins, clever and ambitious. He created the fleet and wanted to fight there, in the strait. Years later, he would be expelled from Athens and would end his days, ironically, in the service of the Persian king.",
        { h: "Eurybiades" },
        "The Spartan admiral who commanded the allied fleet, because the other cities would not accept an Athenian in command. The Peloponnesians wanted to retreat to the Isthmus of Corinth and defend the Peloponnese.",
        { h: "Xerxes I" },
        "He wanted to destroy the Greek fleet and end the war before winter. He watched the battle from a throne on Mount Aigaleo, on the coast, with scribes noting who fought well.",
        { h: "Artemisia" },
        "Queen of Halicarnassus, Herodotus' city, and a ship commander on the Persian side. According to Herodotus, she advised Xerxes not to fight. In the battle, pursued, she sank a ship from her own side to escape. Xerxes, watching from afar, thought it was a Greek ship and said that his men had become women and his women, men."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Herodotus counts 378 Greek ships, Aeschylus, who fought there, 310. Athens alone brought about 180. The Persians had, according to Herodotus and Aeschylus, more than 1,200 ships at the start of the campaign; after the storms and Artemisium, most moderns estimate between 600 and 800 at Salamis (some propose from 400 to 700). Phoenicians, Egyptians, Cypriots and Ionian Greeks made up the bulk.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The strait between Salamis and the coast of Attica is tight and full of bends. There the Persian fleet could neither use its numbers nor maneuver. On open sea, it would be the opposite.",
        { h: "The message" },
        "The allies wanted to flee to the isthmus. Themistocles, according to Herodotus, threatened to take the Athenian fleet and people to Italy if they left. Then he sent his slave Sicinnus to Xerxes, saying that the Greeks were quarreling and would flee at night, and that the Athenians were on the Persian side.",
        "Xerxes believed it. At night, he ordered the exits of the strait closed and placed about 400 soldiers on the islet of Psyttaleia, at the entrance. The Persian rowers spent the whole night rowing on guard. Aristides, Themistocles' rival, came from Aegina slipping through the blockade and confirmed it: they were surrounded. Now the Greeks had to fight.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The battle" },
        "In the morning, the Persian fleet entered the strait in columns: the Phoenicians on the right, on the Attica side, and the Ionians on the left. The Greeks were on the Salamis side. According to Diodorus, Athenians and Spartans were on the left wing, facing the Phoenicians, and Aeginetans and Megarians on the right; Herodotus does not detail the order. On seeing the Greek fleet, some ships backed away, as if fleeing. It was meant to draw the Persians farther in.",
        "According to a tradition cited by Herodotus, the Corinthian fleet sailed north at the start of the battle, perhaps fleeing, perhaps to block the other exit of the strait. The Athenians said it was cowardice; the rest of Greece, that it was a maneuver.",
        "When the Persians were squeezed into the channel, the Greek triremes advanced with the [[ram|esporão]]. The Persian ships in front tried to back off, but those behind kept pushing. In Aeschylus' words, the ships crashed against one another. The Greeks pierced hulls and broke banks of oars. The Athenians broke the Phoenician line; the Aeginetans caught those who fled.",
        "Many Persian sailors could not swim and drowned; the Greeks who fell in the water swam to Salamis. At the end of the day, Aristides landed with hoplites on Psyttaleia and killed the entire Persian force on the islet. Ariabignes, Xerxes' brother and an admiral, also died."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Diodorus says the Greeks lost 40 ships, and the Persians more than 200, not counting those captured; modern reconstructions speak of about 200 to 300 Persian. Herodotus does not give the exact count. The numbers are uncertain, but the Persian fleet lost the ability to dominate the sea. Xerxes went back to Asia, afraid that the Greeks would destroy the bridges over the Hellespont, and left the general Mardonius with an army in Greece. He was defeated at Plataea, the following year.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Salamis saved Athens and, with it, the democracy that would go on to flourish. The rowers of the triremes were the poorest citizens, and the victory gave them political weight. From this was born Athenian naval power, which would lead to the Athenian Empire and the golden age of Pericles. Many historians count it among the most important naval battles in history.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Aeschylus fought in the battle and, eight years later, wrote the play 'The Persians', which tells it from the point of view of the defeated. It is the oldest account we have." },
        { note: "The exact date is debated: it is usually given as the end of September 480 BC (26 or 27 September on English Wikipedia, 29 September on Portuguese), but it is a reconstruction." },
        { note: "According to ancient and late traditions, Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides are linked to the battle: the first fought, the second, as a teenager, sang in the victory chorus, and the third is said to have been born on Salamis on the day of the battle. Only Aeschylus' participation is well attested; the rest is too beautiful to be certain." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

